WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
<v Narrator>The research says something plainly.

2
00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:03.750
You will miss things.

3
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.900
Hook and his colleagues write that missed cultural opportunities and

4
00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:09.250
microaggressions will happen.

5
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.800
What matters is what comes next.

6
00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.350
They name repairing those moments without defensiveness as a core skill.

7
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.500
They also found that people judge their own humility poorly.

8
00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:25.050
You cannot see your blind spots by looking harder at yourself.

9
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.800
That is why feedback from the people you serve, your peers and your supervisor

10
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:32.150
matters so much.

11
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.500
So the skill to build is recovering well.

12
00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:39.850
<v Narrator>A correction gives you information your first interpretation missed.

13
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.800
You might hear it at work as, “That is not why I asked,” or, “The explanation missed

14
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:52.550
what matters to me.” In the friendship story, the second friend says, “I appreciated

15
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.900
the offer.

16
00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:59.650
I wanted to manage the packing because accepting help would make me worry about

17
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:05.400
owing you time you may need.” The first friend's rejection story now has clear

18
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:06.750
contrary information.

19
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:13.300
Look at how the first friend can respond: “I heard the refusal as distance, and I

20
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:18.250
understand that was not what you meant.” That names the specific inference.

21
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.600
It does not apologize for a whole identity.

22
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.350
It does not ask the second friend for comfort.

23
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:31.100
A further offer can stay open, with the second friend free to say no.

24
00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:34.850
A correction is about this person and this moment.

25
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.200
<v Narrator>Repairs tend to go sideways in three familiar ways.

26
00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.750
The first is defending your intention.

27
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.100
You explain at length that you meant well.

28
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:52.450
That may be true, but it does not address what you got wrong, and it can ask the

29
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.000
other person to manage your feelings.

30
00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.550
The second is asking for reassurance.

31
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.100
You need to hear that you are still a good person.

32
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:08.250
That gives the other person more work at the very moment they told you something

33
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.600
hard.

34
00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.150
The third is declaring it fixed.

35
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.100
You apologized, so it must be resolved.

36
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.450
But an apology is an action you took.

37
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:24.200
Its effect on the other person is something you learn from them, over time.

38
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:27.550
A sound repair is smaller and more specific.

39
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.300
Name what you misunderstood.

40
00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.250
Ask whether your new understanding is accurate.

41
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.000
Change what you can.

42
00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.150
Then leave the outcome open.

43
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.500
<v Narrator>Sometimes the hardest part of a repair is that the first interpretation has already

44
00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:43.850
traveled.

45
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.200
One reading describes a service note that calls a person unwilling to involve

46
00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:53.750
others, when the person had only asked for one private conversation.

47
00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:57.900
That broad description could quietly reduce the support offered later.

48
00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.850
The same thing can happen at work.

49
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.600
A newcomer who sent written comments instead of speaking in a meeting gets described

50
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:11.550
as not committed, and that judgment shapes the assignments they are offered.

51
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:15.300
So after a correction, ask where your account went.

52
00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:21.850
Is it in a note, a summary, a planning discussion or someone's memory of a meeting?

53
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.200
If it is, a private change of mind will not undo its effects.

54
00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:32.150
Address it through the responsibility you have, keep the correction specific, and

55
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.500
respect privacy.

56
00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.650
<v Narrator>One boundary needs to be clear.

57
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.600
An ordinary misunderstanding and a formal concern are not the same thing.

58
00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:46.150
If someone alleges harassment or discrimination based on a protected characteristic,

59
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:52.700
a reflective conversation or a private apology is not enough on its own, and it must

60
00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:54.850
never delay the required process.

61
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.400
Under Minnesota Management and Budget Policy 1436, managers and supervisors report

62
00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:04.350
those allegations or incidents immediately to HR or the agency Affirmative Action

63
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.500
Officer and follow agency procedures.

64
00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:13.050
They do not wait for proof, for a group discussion or for their own reflection to

65
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.400
finish.

66
00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.750
The readings give a wider rule as well.

67
00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:23.900
When a right, a complaint, a labor process, a safety duty or Tribal consultation is

68
00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:28.850
involved, contact the person or area responsible and follow its required process.

69
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.800
Humility can shape how respectfully you respond.

70
00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:35.150
It never delays or replaces the duty itself.

71
00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:39.900
<v Narrator>After a misstep, a common reaction is to pull back.

72
00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:45.250
If asking caused trouble, maybe it is safer not to ask at all.

73
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:50.200
In the friendship story, the first friend considers quietly withdrawing and never

74
00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:51.950
raising the offer again.

75
00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:56.900
That avoids an awkward moment, but it leaves the rejection story unexamined.

76
00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:59.450
Avoidance keeps the existing default untouched.

77
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.400
Remember the finding that anxiety reduces curiosity.

78
00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:07.750
The answer is not to force yourself into hard conversations all at once.

79
00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:11.100
It is to build comfort step by step.

80
00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:14.450
Ask one relevant question with permission to decline.

81
00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.400
Notice one opening and respond to it.

82
00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:21.150
Each small, honest attempt makes the next one easier.

